examples.solution_text:
Both curves start at $30$.

$\dfrac{dG}{dt}=0.2G>0$

As $G$ increases, its rate increases, so the growth curve becomes steeper.

$\dfrac{dD}{dt}=-0.2D<0$

As $D$ decreases, the magnitude $0.2D$ of its rate decreases, so the decay curve becomes less steep. It approaches the horizontal asymptote $Q=0$ without reaching it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{growthblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{decayred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\begin{axis}[width=70mm,height=52mm,axis lines=left,xmin=0,xmax=5.5,ymin=0,ymax=90,xlabel={$t$},ylabel={$Q$},xtick={0,1,2,3,4,5},ytick={0,30,60,90},tick label style={font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont,text=black},label style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},axis line style={black},tick style={black},clip=false]
\addplot[color=growthblue,thick,domain=0:5,samples=80] {30*exp(0.2*x)};
\addplot[color=decayred,thick,dashed,domain=0:5,samples=80] {30*exp(-0.2*x)};
\path (axis cs:4.4,78) node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {$G$};
\path (axis cs:4.4,17) node[font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black] {$D$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
